s $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-168896/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb1182f-76ed-514d-808a-94cad16bc2c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168896"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168898/selection/42f0538fd078bbf861fdc638ebbc563ce7ddfcad30ad67672695352c1c080636" :answer/id #uuid "15116dc2-522e-5e62-a131-898c5f0f026c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168898/selection/e82442567e3abcdf41206bbd4ce1bb296d27c169ea26e94a4b10d13fa8b7be8b" :answer/id #uuid "2a4c455f-4e29-54a6-9b22-48753e658e20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168898/selection/ac8a5afb653652ec67cf1dddd8ab35bfd923ef84a3487863b1c326a672d0e543" :answer/id #uuid "37b2667a-8b1e-5fba-9eec-f2350bc4c342" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168898/selection/06dcefa60e00ff9e6d96069cadd86ec0b539353ab96273e37378f99d8f17f78c" :answer/id #uuid "3b0291d7-283a-56e6-b7ac-f79148cbc1fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168898/selection/486fa6265ca67c2f6000c00b1e62fc8e1ef5463726e6dbb0c2b340bb9dd037b6" :answer/id #uuid "73d566aa-eda2-546c-83b0-4b9003fbedad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168898/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8dbcc958-599e-576f-9487-b626865a1b38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-168898/selection/42f0538fd078bbf861fdc638ebbc563ce7ddfcad30ad67672695352c1c080636" :answer-field/choices ["q-168898/selection/42f0538fd078bbf861fdc638ebbc563ce7ddfcad30ad67672695352c1c080636" "q-168898/selection/e82442567e3abcdf41206bbd4ce1bb296d27c169ea26e94a4b10d13fa8b7be8b" "q-168898/selection/ac8a5afb653652ec67cf1dddd8ab35bfd923ef84a3487863b1c326a672d0e543" "q-168898/selection/06dcefa60e00ff9e6d96069cadd86ec0b539353ab96273e37378f99d8f17f78c" "q-168898/selection/486fa6265ca67c2f6000c00b1e62fc8e1ef5463726e6dbb0c2b340bb9dd037b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168898" :question/id #uuid "0699ef4a-46bc-50f3-8bab-c13f8e76f124" :question/math-academy-id "q-168898" :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-2,2,2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-2,2)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-2,2,2)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-2,2).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-2)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(-2,2)$ lies in the second quadrant, we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{2}{-2}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-168898/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168898"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-74479/selection/299184030f5331f976847de0be24d81352a6f5fdf729254e4d2d19d159262ec2" :answer/id #uuid "3930bb12-d564-51ce-ba01-3b4f0206a904" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5},1.571,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74479/selection/42a031a1b0785d00a58e0f9e7b96a2eec9a4c4ea03f2097c5405972897af5580" :answer/id #uuid "7c251fc3-ee38-5557-8023-971cd62d5ac5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5,2.214,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74479/selection/1a8338c9bf894202324226c84708a6fb3c4b2f0f3047ae853a25559a62bc4741" :answer/id #uuid "2065a636-3bd8-5201-977a-317244519985" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5},3.141,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74479/selection/c3bcdfc4edce827105378fb4f86db319f96c70b6c2fe89808db04d59c08c8494" :answer/id #uuid "321617ec-a2ed-5746-9da6-e66b5966a4f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5,0.785,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74479/selection/8460c6f2fc5e9e9b9d97b3e32dbf1348a14f7f05db0787ad846b3f8aa568a56a" :answer/id #uuid "394e1e0d-82eb-5691-95de-25572ca9515e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{10},3.141,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74479/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3301c95f-3317-5628-8466-1e3972182a86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-74479/selection/42a031a1b0785d00a58e0f9e7b96a2eec9a4c4ea03f2097c5405972897af5580" :answer-field/choices ["q-74479/selection/299184030f5331f976847de0be24d81352a6f5fdf729254e4d2d19d159262ec2" "q-74479/selection/42a031a1b0785d00a58e0f9e7b96a2eec9a4c4ea03f2097c5405972897af5580" "q-74479/selection/1a8338c9bf894202324226c84708a6fb3c4b2f0f3047ae853a25559a62bc4741" "q-74479/selection/c3bcdfc4edce827105378fb4f86db319f96c70b6c2fe89808db04d59c08c8494" "q-74479/selection/8460c6f2fc5e9e9b9d97b3e32dbf1348a14f7f05db0787ad846b3f8aa568a56a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74479" :question/id #uuid "2cc9f6fd-6cbc-5289-99ad-a9d6b26e5b99" :question/math-academy-id "q-74479" :question/problem "The point $C$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-3,4,-1).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ,z)$ of $C?$ Express $\\theta $ in radians rounded to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ \n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-3,4)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-3,4,-1)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-3,4).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $C$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(-3,4)$ lies in the second quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{4}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.927\\,295\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi -0.927\\,295 \\\\  & =2.214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $C$ are $(5,2.214,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-74479/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74479"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-74482/selection/dce00d7ce8204e18201dfe0c2af9fee9df86f719af24e4d05a29f199596e1e96" :answer/id #uuid "2822ceec-5e60-5acd-ae73-6eb49761f406" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74482/selection/c3d33785e2d8b38165404b34f83c0255aff9a3018bfd3e9cb938abe1dbde4189" :answer/id #uuid "60b0435b-a8bf-5fcd-a656-01b976ac568a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74482/selection/89a7756eefb8b931634fe22043581b470f2205b64a7fe8af3045f359bb6040b9" :answer/id #uuid "eba853e7-2c91-5f73-bc90-a114a4866028" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74482/selection/005fbc5ff3421bb0e72567ccf55c897e5b184c2e67358636f512510def7a31b1" :answer/id #uuid "f62909b4-7946-5032-917f-c6ded9d81a96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74482/selection/924780c45d5faf0d32881a79adcdc79914aff0948cc47d3fd945951b71c7d1a5" :answer/id #uuid "374cad50-3ed0-57a6-bb68-8586e90e0d93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74482/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d20f7b61-16da-5d4a-aec6-c9ade57c8a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-74482/selection/89a7756eefb8b931634fe22043581b470f2205b64a7fe8af3045f359bb6040b9" :answer-field/choices ["q-74482/selection/dce00d7ce8204e18201dfe0c2af9fee9df86f719af24e4d05a29f199596e1e96" "q-74482/selection/c3d33785e2d8b38165404b34f83c0255aff9a3018bfd3e9cb938abe1dbde4189" "q-74482/selection/89a7756eefb8b931634fe22043581b470f2205b64a7fe8af3045f359bb6040b9" "q-74482/selection/005fbc5ff3421bb0e72567ccf55c897e5b184c2e67358636f512510def7a31b1" "q-74482/selection/924780c45d5faf0d32881a79adcdc79914aff0948cc47d3fd945951b71c7d1a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74482" :question/id #uuid "1f1545fd-077a-5d15-8508-132b073ff140" :question/math-academy-id "q-74482" :question/problem "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(3,-\\sqrt{3},-3).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ \n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(3,-\\sqrt{3})$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(3,-\\sqrt{3},-3)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $P$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(3,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{3}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-74482/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74482"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-74297"] :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(2,-2,5).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(2,-2)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,5)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(2,-2).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(2,-2)$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2}{2}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4},5).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-74478/selection/faa0f9500062c0c375881b3a1424665e7cd1b555205facda1446c5916dddc84e" :answer/id #uuid "d7281004-716f-59b6-9c04-f82fe04088f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74478/selection/3c4634049638bcd19324beb59779a92b287065063b071480b4f636748587e103" :answer/id #uuid "b8fce348-ddfa-55f7-9dad-496a9fa435c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74478/selection/8463e3c1a9c9a75cab85acacf50cb8a75f7b5f8c3db432e30f07a2dc1089b7c5" :answer/id #uuid "95befea1-e010-520e-984e-1f142b758c3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74478/selection/a54791d91d715f9975e83a843b043b52aa04377891a111f19f07b89d797cbb8c" :answer/id #uuid "99e1c33a-5677-5bbe-9e9a-13d32494e7d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74478/selection/025e747c6deff64606bc3eb1ef708f87cd844c69ba96b2afe4d02c880d1e41d4" :answer/id #uuid "8be40b28-bc94-5725-b520-bd13f6a6497e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74478/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3bac72af-705c-53cc-a679-e95f5e2f1ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-74478/selection/8463e3c1a9c9a75cab85acacf50cb8a75f7b5f8c3db432e30f07a2dc1089b7c5" :answer-field/choices ["q-74478/selection/faa0f9500062c0c375881b3a1424665e7cd1b555205facda1446c5916dddc84e" "q-74478/selection/3c4634049638bcd19324beb59779a92b287065063b071480b4f636748587e103" "q-74478/selection/8463e3c1a9c9a75cab85acacf50cb8a75f7b5f8c3db432e30f07a2dc1089b7c5" "q-74478/selection/a54791d91d715f9975e83a843b043b52aa04377891a111f19f07b89d797cbb8c" "q-74478/selection/025e747c6deff64606bc3eb1ef708f87cd844c69ba96b2afe4d02c880d1e41d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74478" :question/id #uuid "df5c8142-fcaf-5796-8871-1ba977c3d88a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74478" :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1,-\\sqrt{3},2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-1,-\\sqrt{3},2)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-1,-\\sqrt{3}).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$ \n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the third  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{-1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the reference angle and $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-74478/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74478"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6219"] :question/problem "What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $N?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/53fada6c8e77c7c6fa2a6579b52af21ea03839e134fc2cb3d9529f6cfc01bd4b.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where $r \\geq 0$ and $0 \\leq \\theta <         2\\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/d60214a4b32c4462d422372aa90e78944649811ce0b1e277f07fe90663e5be94.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through $N'$ makes an angle of $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to $N'$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=3$ because $N$ lies $3$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $N$ are $\\left(\\sqrt{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, 3\\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6221"] :question/problem "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $\\left(4, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, -8 \\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt3, 2, -8).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6220"] :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where $r \\geq 0$ and $0 \\leq \\theta <    2\\pi.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are given as $(x,y,z) = (\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2).$\n\n- From these Cartesian coordinates, we know that $z=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- To find the values of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we need to find the polar coordinates of the point $(x,y) = (\\sqrt 3, -3)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nFirst, we find $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(\\sqrt3,-3)$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle $\\theta_R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}, \\sqrt2 \\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
